==================================== INPUT ====================================
// Test: a nested `@UNSTABLE_key` field set is rejected, not parsed as best it can be.
//
// The composite-schemas spec allows `fields: "actor_key { id }"`. Relay accepts
// only flat names. Splitting this one on whitespace would yield `actor_key`,
// `{`, `id`, `}` — granting `actor_key` outright, when the author wrote it only
// to qualify a nested selection. Granting more than was asked for is the one
// outcome an escape hatch must never produce, so the malformed set is an error.
//- FooQuery.js
graphql`query FooQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  me {
    greeting
  }
}`

//- User_greeting.js
/**
 * @relayField User.greeting: RelayResolverValue
 * @rootFragment UserGreetingFragment
 */
graphql`fragment UserGreetingFragment on User {
  id
  actor_key
}`

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "flow",
  "jsModuleFormat": "haste",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "schemaExtensions": ["schema-extensions"],
  "featureFlags": {
    "enable_exec_time_resolvers_directive": true
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query {
  me: User
}

type User {
  id: ID!
  name: String
  actor_key: ID!
}

//- schema-extensions/extension.graphql
extend type User @UNSTABLE_key(fields: "actor_key { id }")
==================================== OUTPUT ===================================
✖︎ Unsupported `@UNSTABLE_key(fields: "actor_key { id }")` on `User`. Relay accepts only a space-separated list of field names on the annotated type — no nested selections, arguments or aliases. Parsing `actor_key { id }` by splitting it would grant fields that were only meant to qualify a selection, so it is rejected instead.

  User_greeting.js:7:3
    6 │   id
    7 │   actor_key
      │   ^^^^^^^^^
    8 │ }
